#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t39-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t93
REASONING=The sentence is: main clause "nam ... celeriter antecellere omnibus ingeni gloria coepit", with a preceding temporal clause "ut primum ex pueris excessit Archias atque ... se ad scribendi studium contulit" (two coordinated verbs, *excessit* and *contulit*, both governed by "ut (primum)"), inside which sits a relative clause "quibus aetas puerilis ad humanitatem informari solet" (*informari* is a complementary infinitive with *solet*, so it gets no verbal-expression entry of its own). A parenthetical clause introduced by *nam* — "nam ibi natus est loco nobili" — is an independent compound (deponent) perfect, anchored at the participle *natus* with *est* as auxiliary. *antecellere* is a complementary infinitive with *coepit* (no verbal-expression entry); *scribendi* is a gerund in the genitive depending on *studium*. Judgment calls flagged: - *adfluenti* (4.t85): it is coordinated by *atque* with the plain adjectives *celebri* and *copiosa* describing *urbe*, which argues for a purely attributive reading; but it governs two ablatives of its own (*hominibus*, *studiis*) and is genuinely participial in force ("overflowing with..."). Per the instruction to prefer the circumstantial reading when uncertain, I treat it as a predicate/circumstantial participle anchoring its own verbal expression, agreeing with *urbe*. - *Antiochiae* (4.t65) is a locative; there is no dedicated locative label, and its appositive *urbe* is ablative (locative-ablative), so I relate *Antiochiae* to *coepit* with 'ablative'. An alternative would be to leave it unrelated. - *celeriter* (4.t87) could modify either *antecellere* or *coepit*; I attach it to the finite verb *coepit*. - *quibus* (4.t50) is an ablative of means; I attach it to *informari* (the infinitive it actually construes with) rather than to *solet*. - *que* (4.t83), though enclitic on *liberalissimis*, functionally joins the noun phrases *hominibus* and *studiis*.

